WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.072 --> 00:00:04.260
People have been keeping bees for many centuries.

2
00:00:04.261 --> 00:00:11.467
In fact, it is one of the oldest forms of agriculture, dating back to 13,000 BC.

3
00:00:11.468 --> 00:00:19.571
Traditionally, beekeepers kept beehives in a special location, called an apiary, for the purpose of collecting honey.

4
00:00:19.572 --> 00:00:25.060
The Ancient Egyptians considered honey to be the most precious gift one could offer.

5
00:00:25.061 --> 00:00:29.764
In northern Europe, honey was known as the "food of the gods."

6
00:00:29.765 --> 00:00:42.754
Today, beekeepers maintain large apiaries and produce many kinds of products, such as beeswax for making candles and royal jelly for consuming as a nutritional supplement.

7
00:00:42.755 --> 00:00:48.326
They even harvest a natural kind of medicine that helps fight disease.

8
00:00:48.327 --> 00:00:59.467
Another important reason people keep bees is not for honey or any other product: bees are vital players in the complex process that allows plants to reproduce.

9
00:00:59.468 --> 00:01:08.202
When a bee visits a blossoming flower, a substance called pollen collects on its body, which the bee carries to the next flower.

10
00:01:08.203 --> 00:01:18.199
Pollen, a fine powder—like material, is the means by which one plant sends its genetic information to another plant, enabling it to produce seeds.

11
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.909
This process is called pollination, and bees provide an enormous help.

12
00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:31.291
Without the assistance of bees, plants would have to rely on random breezes to carry the pollen from one plant to another.

13
00:01:31.292 --> 00:01:35.854
In fact, bees do about 75% of the work.

14
00:01:35.855 --> 00:01:45.440
Farmers usually keep apiaries on their land or hire migrant beekeepers, who bring bees to their farms when crops are ready for pollination.

15
00:01:45.441 --> 00:01:49.699
But why do bees travel so far, visiting so many plants?

16
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:57.579
Because honey, which is their most important source of food, is made from the sugary nectar found in flower blossoms.

17
00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.514
Bees collect the nectar from hundreds of flowers and bring it back to their hive.

18
00:02:02.515 --> 00:02:09.236
A hive is a natural house that a colony of bees builds to protect themselves and their babies.

19
00:02:09.237 --> 00:02:17.560
Within the hive, the bees deposit the nectar into tiny pockets, and add a few special chemicals that they secrete.

20
00:02:17.561 --> 00:02:23.797
Then, they beat their wings fiercely, creating a strong wind that blows through the hive.

21
00:02:23.798 --> 00:02:30.741
This draws moisture away from the nectar, thickening the mixture, and the result is a gooey, sweet honey.

22
00:02:30.742 --> 00:02:35.341
The bees use this honey to feed their babies and themselves.

23
00:02:35.348 --> 00:02:45.604
People also enjoy eating honey, so when humans began to domesticate bees, they searched for ways to produce more honey than the bees needed to survive.

24
00:02:45.606 --> 00:02:54.701
The easiest way to do this was to provide the bees with an artificial hive so that they didn't have to spend so much energy building one on their own.

25
00:02:54.702 --> 00:03:02.751
A modern artificial hive is designed to be comfortable for the bees and accessible for the beekeepers, who need to remove the honey.

26
00:03:02.752 --> 00:03:09.029
It is also portable, so it can be moved from one apiary to another without too much trouble.

27
00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:15.020
Each hive has between five and ten wooden boxes that can be stacked one on top of the other.

28
00:03:15.021 --> 00:03:20.962
Within each box, frames are stored vertically, like files in a filing cabinet.

29
00:03:20.963 --> 00:03:26.469
The bees use the frames to build hexagonal honeycombs out of natural beeswax.

30
00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:33.538
Then, the bees happily use the tiny pockets in the honeycomb to deposit their nectar and make honey.

31
00:03:33.539 --> 00:03:43.296
Once all the honeycombs in one box are filled with honey, beekeepers put on special protective suits made from tough nylon material.

32
00:03:43.297 --> 00:03:52.716
These "bee suits" feature a veiled hood with a wire mesh screen that covers the face, so bees can't get into the suit to sting the beekeeper.

33
00:03:52.717 --> 00:03:58.494
At the hive, the beekeeper uses a device called a smoker to chase away the bees.

34
00:03:58.495 --> 00:04:03.594
The smoker is a machine that contains small amounts of burning sawdust or pinecones.

35
00:04:03.595 --> 00:04:07.193
With it, the beekeeper directs smoke at the hive.

36
00:04:07.194 --> 00:04:11.701
Amazingly, the bees do not panic, but instead become calmer.

37
00:04:11.702 --> 00:04:17.552
They leave the apiary to avoid the smoke, making it possible for the beekeeper to approach the hive.

38
00:04:17.553 --> 00:04:21.779
Any bees that remain on the honeycombs can be brushed away gently.

39
00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.937
The beekeeper removes the box from the hive, lifts out the full honeycombs, and then replaces them with empty ones.

40
00:04:28.938 --> 00:04:34.290
The full honeycombs are taken to a processing area and the honey is harvested.

41
00:04:34.291 --> 00:04:40.012
Bees cap their honeycombs with a layer of the same beeswax from which the honeycombs are made.

42
00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:47.942
To remove the honey, beekeepers heat a common bread knife to easily slice through the caps and open up the tiny pockets.

43
00:04:47.943 --> 00:04:51.653
The open honeycombs are put into an extracting machine.

44
00:04:51.654 --> 00:05:00.454
In the same way that a washing machine spins to remove water from damp clothes, the extracting machine spins the honeycomb to remove the honey.

45
00:05:00.455 --> 00:05:05.111
The honey is flung against the sides of the machine and collected in the drain.

46
00:05:05.112 --> 00:05:16.962
But, the honey that is caught contains chunks of wax and even parts of bees, such as legs and wings—not an appetizing prospect when you spread honey on your toast at breakfast.

47
00:05:16.963 --> 00:05:24.719
So, beekeepers must do one final step: they pour the honey through a large filter to make it pure.

48
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:31.147
Pure honey is delicious and beekeepers are always trying to find new ways to improve its quality.

49
00:05:31.148 --> 00:05:35.486
For example, beekeepers have experimented with different flavors.

50
00:05:35.487 --> 00:05:40.728
The flavor of the honey that bees make depends on the type of plant from which they take their nectar.

51
00:05:40.729 --> 00:05:47.173
As a result, there are many varieties of honey to suit the individual tastes of people all over the world.

52
00:05:47.174 --> 00:05:54.091
Honey made from alfalfa nectar is very popular in the United States, as is honey made from tupelo.

53
00:05:54.092 --> 00:05:59.365
Another favorite is orange blossom honey, which has a sweet citrus taste.

54
00:05:59.366 --> 00:06:06.652
In some cases, beekeepers also feed their bees hard candy that has been melted down and rolled into sheets.

55
00:06:06.653 --> 00:06:16.066
This provides the bees with a reliable food source in times when natural food is unavailable, and can also make the honey taste sweeter.

56
00:06:16.067 --> 00:06:22.828
With all the positive benefits that bees bring, it's difficult to believe that people are so scared of being stung by them.

57
00:06:22.829 --> 00:06:25.836
Still, the fear is understandable.

58
00:06:25.837 --> 00:06:32.335
In 1957, beekeepers from Brazil introduced a few bees from Africa to their local hives.

59
00:06:32.336 --> 00:06:41.227
They hoped that the African bees would breed with the European bees they were using and produce hybrids that would be better able to survive in the hot climate.

60
00:06:41.228 --> 00:06:46.566
In any hive, the queen bee is the only female who is able to lay eggs.

61
00:06:46.567 --> 00:06:54.496
When the queen bees from Africa took over the Brazilian hives, they bred with the weaker bees and produced aggressive hybrids.

62
00:06:54.498 --> 00:06:58.479
Within a few years, the hybrids had taken over the apiaries.

63
00:06:58.487 --> 00:07:03.049
A few bee colonies attacked their beekeepers and fatalities were reported.

64
00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.502
The new bees soon earned the nickname "killer" bees.

65
00:07:06.503 --> 00:07:11.221
Efforts to tame domesticated killer bees, however, have been successful.

66
00:07:11.222 --> 00:07:18.435
Very aggressive hives have their queens removed, and beekeepers substitute a queen known to be less aggressive.

67
00:07:18.436 --> 00:07:22.599
The majority of bees are not interested in stinging people at all.

68
00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:28.540
They are busy looking for flower blossoms, collecting nectar, and delivering it to their hives.

69
00:07:28.541 --> 00:07:32.989
They work hard to pollinate plants so that humans can grow enough food.

70
00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:38.601
Under the watchful eye of beekeepers, bees continue to provide us with delightful honey.

